<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abbey of Saint-Médard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century</span> · Also known as Saint-Médard Abbey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Abbey of Saint-Médard was a Benedictine monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, founded in the 6th century over the tomb of Saint Médard, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Noyon. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar I</a> was buried there in 561, as was his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigebert-i">Sigebert I</a>, and it became one of the richest abbeys of Merovingian and Carolingian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> was held there by his rebellious sons in 833.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-medard.jpg" alt="Abbey of Saint-Médard" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chris06 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soissons_Krypta1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
